===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable|uncountable}} Any of numerous [[hemiparasitic]] [[evergreen]] [[plant#Noun|plants]] of the [[order#Noun|order]] [[Santalales]] with [[white]] [[berry|berries]] that grow in the [[crown#Noun|crowns]] of [[apple tree]]s, [[oak]]s, and other [[tree]]s, such as the [[European mistletoe]] (''[[Viscum album]]'') and {{vern|American mistletoe}} or {{vern|eastern mistletoe}} ({{taxlink|Phoradendron leucarpum|species}}).
#* {{RQ:Chambers Cyclopaedia|edition=2nd|entry=MISTLETOE|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=DHOj9vGhuroC&pg=PP149|column=1|passage='''''Miſtletoe''''', by phyſicians, &c. called ''viſcus'', grows to the height of about two feet. {{...}} [[w:Pliny the Elder|Pliny]] and moſt naturaliſts relate, that thruſhes being exceedingly fond of the berries of the '''''miſtletoe'''''; they ſwallow them, and caſt them out again on the branches of trees, where they uſe to perch; and by this means give occaſion to a new production of '''''miſtletoe'''''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Robert Mallet, Jun.|title=Art. IV. Horticultural Jottanda of a Recent Continental Tour.|editor=[[w:John Claudius Loudon|J[ohn] C[laudius] Loudon]]|magazine=The Gardener’s Magazine, and Register of Rural & Domestic Improvement|location=London|publisher=Printed for [[w:Longman|Longman, Rees, Orme, Brown, Green, and Longman]],{{nb...|Paternoster-Row.}}|month=February|year=1833|volume=IX|issue=42|page=27|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=R-8oEIREqRMC&pg=PA28|oclc=863445450|passage=In Martigny I first observed the custom, common throughout many parts of Switzerland, of hanging '''mistletoes''' over the house doors; a superstition for which I have not been able to get any reason rendered.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=James Backhouse|authorlink=James Backhouse|chapter=XXX|title=A Narrative of a Visit to the Mauritius and South Africa|location=London|publisher=Hamilton, Adams, and Co.,{{nb...|Paternoster Row}}; York: John L. Linney,{{nb...|Low Ousegate.}}|year=1844|page=534|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=k2lCAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA534|oclc=1007222909|passage='''Mistletoes''' were growing on the Euphorbias; they were much more succulent than when growing on woody shrubs or on trees. {{...}} One of the '''Mistletoes''' common in Southern Africa, has small, myrtle-like leaves and red berries; another is leafless, and has stems resembling a ''Salicornia''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Robert O. Tinnin|author2=Donald M. Knutson|chapter=Types of Dwarf Mistletoe Brooms|title=How to Identify Brooms in Douglas-fir Caused by Dwarf Mistletoe|series=United States Department of Agriculture Research Note|seriesvolume=PNW 426|location=[Portland, Or.?]|publisher=Pacific Northwest Forest and Range Experiment Station, [[w:United States Forest Service|Forest Service]], {{w|United States Department of Agriculture}}|month=May|year=1985|page=2|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=zQoCexpyhVsC&pg=PP2|oclc=12593932|passage=The brooms caused by Douglas-fir dwarf '''mistletoe''' begin as small sprays of twigs radiating from a swollen limb {{...}} but in time they become large, more or less spherical in shape, and often with long, droopy twigs. {{...}} Although the parasite is present throughout each broom, the only visible portions of dwarf '''mistletoe''' are the reproductive shoots which are called "aerial shoots."}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Olivia Judson|authorlink=Olivia Judson|title=The hemiparasite season [print version: Under the hemiparasite, ''International New York Times'', 24–25 December 2014, page&nbsp;7]|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/opinion/the-hemiparasite-season.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20141223181330/https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/opinion/the-hemiparasite-season.html|archivedate=23 December 2014|newspaper={{w|The New York Times}}|date=23 December 2014|passage=[T]he stickiness [of mistletoe berries] persists through the gut, making it hard to defecate '''mistletoe''' seeds with any grace. To deal with this, birds like the [[w:silky-flycatcher|silky flycatcher]], Phainopepla nitens, that are '''mistletoe''' specialists, have evolved a "waggle dance"&nbsp;– also known as butt-wiping behavior&nbsp;– where they clean up by rubbing themselves against twigs. Indeed, the association with bird droppings may even be the origin of the word: Some evidence suggests that "'''mistletoe'''" comes from Anglo-Saxon for "dung on a twig."}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A [[sprig]] of one such plant used as a [[Christmas]] [[decoration]], associated with the [[custom#Noun|custom]] that a [[man#Noun|man]] may [[kiss#Verb|kiss]] any [[woman]] [[stand#Verb|standing]] beneath it.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Benjamin Martin|authorlink=Benjamin Martin (lexicographer)|chapter=Dialogue VI. Reflections on the Wonderful Nature, and Singular Properties of Some Kinds of Plants, Shrubs, and Trees; Exemplified in Missltoe,{{sic}} Sensitive Plant, Bulbous Garlic, Bee-orchis, and Egg-plant.|title=The Young Gentleman and Lady’s Philosophy in a Continued Survey of the Works of Nature and Art; by Way of Dialogue|location=London|publisher=Printed for W. Owen,{{nb...|No. 11, near Temple-Bar}}; and the author,{{nb...|at his house in Fleet-Street.}}|year=1782|volume=III, part IV (Containing a General Survey of Vegetation and Vegetables)|page=229|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=z3gKAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA229|oclc=84791143|passage='''Miſtletoe'''! why I know what that is, as we garniſh our Houſe with it every Year, at ''Chriſtmas''.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|chapter=Stave Three. The Second of the Three Spirits.|pages=81–82|pageref=82|passage=There were ruddy, brown-faced, broad-girthed Spanish Onions, shining in the fatness of their growth like Spanish Friars; and winking from their shelves in wanton slyness at the girls as they went by, and glanced demurely at the hung-up '''mistletoe'''.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author=[[w:Robert Wells (songwriter)|Robert Wells]]; {{w|Mel Tormé}}|title={{w|The Christmas Song}}|artist={{w|Nat King Cole}}|month=November|year=1946|passage=Everybody knows a turkey and some '''mistletoe''' / Help to make the season bright / Tiny tots with their eyes all aglow / Will find it hard to sleep tonight}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author=Johnny Marks|authorlink=Johnny Marks|title=[[w:Rockin' Around the Christmas Tree|Rockin’ Around the Christmas Tree]]|album=Merry Christmas from Brenda Lee|artist={{w|Brenda Lee}}|year=1958|passage=Rockin' around the Christmas tree / At the Christmas party hop / '''Mistletoe''' hung where you can see / Every couple tries to stop}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Olivia Judson|authorlink=Olivia Judson|title=The hemiparasite season [print version: Under the hemiparasite, ''International New York Times'', 24–25 December 2014, page&nbsp;7]|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/opinion/the-hemiparasite-season.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20141223181330/https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/opinion/the-hemiparasite-season.html|archivedate=23 December 2014|newspaper={{w|The New York Times}}|date=23 December 2014|passage='''Mistletoe'''. The word conjures winter holidays, office parties, stolen kisses, romance. But appreciation of the plant is no modern thing. Kissing under the '''mistletoe''' has been happening for at least 200 years. And some 2,000 years ago, the druids in which is now Britain venerated the plant when it grew on an oak. When they found it, they dressed in white, harvested it with a golden sickle, and sacrificed two white bulls. Or so says that great Roman, {{w|Pliny the Elder}}, in his "[[w:Natural History (Pliny)|Natural History]]."}}

